var documenterSearchIndex = {"docs":
[{"category":"section","location":"codebooks/#Codebooks-and-provenance","page":"Codebooks and provenance","text":"","title":"Codebooks and provenance"},{"category":"section","location":"codebooks/#Choose-a-source-explicitly","page":"Codebooks and provenance","text":"using HondurasTools\nbundled = load_codebook()\ncb = load_codebook(path=joinpath(ENV[\"HONDURAS_CODEBOOK\"], \"codebook\"))\n\nThe path must contain variables.csv, options.csv, and derivations.csv; it is not the external repository root. The local processed repository is /Users/emf/Documents/Yale/yale research/honduras/codebooks honduras. Set HONDURAS_CODEBOOK to that root in your shell to use the second example. Loading a codebook does not automatically change cleaner behavior; pass it to functions that accept it, such as clean_outcomes(...; codebook=cb).\n\nThe external repository declares version 2026.5.1 as inspected on 2026-11-04. Its codebook/datapackage.json and codebook/schema.md define the schema; guides/ explains study design, identifiers, survey administration, and longitudinal changes. Consult those guides for the meaning of survey rather than assuming all baseline questionnaires were administered to everyone.","title":"Choose a source explicitly"},{"category":"section","location":"codebooks/#Tables-and-lookups","page":"Codebooks and provenance","text":"Table Unit\nvariables Study, variable, and wave\noptions Study, variable, wave, and option code\nderivations Derived/outcome/treatment variable, with construction notes\n\nThe loader returns raw DataFrames, grouped indexes, and an outcome-type lookup. Queries default to study=\"rct\"; specify \"ih\" or \"hmb\" explicitly when appropriate.\n\nvariable_info(cb, \"a0200\"; study=\"rct\", wave=3)\nvariable_options(cb, \"a0200\"; study=\"rct\", wave=3)\nvariables_by_level(cb, \"respondent\"; study=\"rct\")\nvariables_by_type(cb, \"outcome\"; study=\"rct\")\n\nOption codes reflect display order, which need not be analytical order. make_ordered_categorical uses English labels in option-code order; verify labels against cleaned values and select a wave when labels or their ordering differ across waves. Existing cleaners may change case, wording, or coding.","title":"Tables and lookups"},{"category":"section","location":"codebooks/#Study-and-wave-scope","page":"Codebooks and provenance","text":"RCT covers waves 1–4. HMB uses wave 1 for cohort 1 baseline, wave 2 for cohort 1 follow-up, wave 3 for cohort 2, and wave 0 for cross-administration derived scores. These HMB values are metadata conventions and are not interchangeable with RCT waves or the cleaner's cohort column. IH is a separate study. The external cooperation and CSS guides are stubs without corresponding codebook rows.","title":"Study and wave scope"},{"category":"section","location":"codebooks/#Bundled-snapshot-and-divergence","page":"Codebooks and provenance","text":"The canonical v2026.5.1 correction normalizes 64 OCR-damaged outcome classifications. All three bundled CSVs now match the canonical files byte-for-byte, without changing the bundled data. codebook/provenance.json records SHA-256 checksums and the source release commit. The loader retains known OCR aliases for older versions and rejects unknown outcome types.\n\nThe bundled directory also contains older reformatted and per-wave CSVs and reformatting scripts. These are historical resources, not inputs to load_codebook(). The provenance manifest covers only the three machine-readable CSVs, not the historical files.\n\nFor reproducibility, record the package commit, codebook release/commit, file checksums, study, and wave selection. Keep canonical metadata edits in the external repository and validate them there with python scripts/validate.py. Reconcile bundled fixes before synchronizing. Do not regenerate the external codebook casually: its extraction pipeline can overwrite manual corrections.","title":"Bundled snapshot and divergence"},{"category":"section","location":"codebooks/#Study-and-wave-specific-outcome-recoding","page":"Codebooks and provenance","text":"clean_outcomes(dfs, waves; codebook=cb, study=\"rct\") matches derivations by study, base variable, and row wave. An unsuffixed derivation applies across waves. Conflicting classifications for the same key raise an error. If an outcome is known for the selected study but has nonmissing values in an undocumented wave, recoding stops with an error rather than borrowing another wave's classification.\n\noutcome_type_index contains the full tuple-keyed lookup. outcome_type_map remains a compatibility view of unambiguous RCT classifications and is no longer used for recoding. Legacy derivation DataFrames without a study column are interpreted as the explicitly selected study.","title":"Study- and wave-specific outcome recoding"},{"category":"section","location":"api/#API-reference","page":"API reference","text":"The reference below is generated from docstrings in the loaded module. Some exported analysis helpers have no docstrings yet; consult their source before use.","title":"API reference"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#HondurasTools.add_building_info!-Tuple{Any, Any}","page":"API reference","text":"Add building ID information for alter1 and alter2 in a network DataFrame.\n\nThis function:\n\nJoins respondent building IDs to both alter1 and alter2 columns\nCreates a same_building indicator variable\n\nParameters:\n\ndf: DataFrame containing alter1 and alter2 columns\nrespondentdf: DataFrame containing name and buildingid columns\nreturn_df: Bool, if true returns the modified DataFrame (default: false)\n\nReturns:\n\nThe modified DataFrame if return_df is true, otherwise nothing\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"HondurasTools.add_building_info!"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#HondurasTools.addneighbors!-NTuple{4, Any}","page":"API reference","text":"addneighbors!(df, nds, r, sl)\n\nDescription\n\nFor an input DataFrame, df, find the set of matching neighbors stored in nds for specified relationship, given by relname.\n\nNeighbor properties, specified in sl, are added from DataFrame r.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"HondurasTools.addneighbors!"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#HondurasTools.addneighbors-NTuple{4, Any}","page":"API reference","text":"addneighbors(cr, ndf4, rhv4, sl)\n\nsl variables to select in rhv4.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"HondurasTools.addneighbors"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#HondurasTools.addnetworkdata!-Tuple{Any, Any}","page":"API reference","text":"    addnetworkdata!(df, ndf)\n\nDescription\n\nAdd the network data.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"HondurasTools.addnetworkdata!"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#HondurasTools.addsymmetric!-Tuple{Any}","page":"API reference","text":"    addsymmetric!(con)\n\nDescription\n\nAdd a column to the con edgelist that indicates whether the ties are recipricated. (In constrast to using non-unique, this marks both directions.)\n\nAlso adds column indicating whether the alter appears as an ego.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"HondurasTools.addsymmetric!"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#HondurasTools.applystandards!-Tuple{Any, Any}","page":"API reference","text":"Description\n\nApply the data standardization for all columns present in transforms.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"HondurasTools.applystandards!"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#HondurasTools.binarize!-Tuple{Any, Any}","page":"API reference","text":"    binarize!(cr, v)\n\nValues == yes -> true; other values -> false. Missing values are retained as missing.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"HondurasTools.binarize!"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#HondurasTools.clean_connections-Tuple{Vector{DataFrame}, Any}","page":"API reference","text":"clean_connections(conns::Vector{DataFrame}, waves;\n                  alter_source=false, same_village=true, removemissing=true)\n\nCombine and clean connection tables in the same order as waves. Mutates inputs. alter_source=true keeps raw source code 1; same_village=true drops missing same-village indicators and keeps code 1. Source codes are then converted to labels. removemissing=true rejects remaining missing values in ego, alter, question, alter source, and relationship using disallowmissing!; it does not drop those rows. Required delivery columns must be present.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"HondurasTools.clean_connections"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#HondurasTools.clean_household-Tuple{Vector{DataFrame}, Any}","page":"API reference","text":"    clean_household(hh, waves; nokeymiss = true)\n\nClean the household level data. hh must be a vector of dataframes. Data must be ordered by and match waves.\n\nARGS ≡≡≡≡≡≡≡≡≡≡\n\nhh: a vector of DataFrames, with entries for each wave of the data.\nwaves: indicate the wave of each DataFrame in the same order as resp.\nnokeymiss = true : whether to filter to not missing on key variables: village code and building id\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"HondurasTools.clean_household"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#HondurasTools.clean_ihr-Tuple{Any, Any}","page":"API reference","text":"    clean_ihr(cop, ihr)\n\nClean the Intellectual Humility Project and the cooperation experiment data. Possibly subject to change when the data is updated.\n\nNOT adjusted for datasets with a limited set of requested variables.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"HondurasTools.clean_ihr"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#HondurasTools.clean_microbiome-Tuple{Any, Any}","page":"API reference","text":"    clean_microbiome(mb1, mb2)\n\nClean the microbiome data, retaining the union of cohort columns and filling absent values with missing. Adds cohort (1 or 2); this is not an HMB wave ID. Donation amounts accept integers or integer strings; absent donation columns are allowed.\n\nARGS ≡≡≡≡≡≡≡≡≡≡\n\nmb1: cohort 1 raw data\nmb2: cohort 2 raw data\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"HondurasTools.clean_microbiome"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#HondurasTools.clean_microbiome-Tuple{Vector{DataFrame}, AbstractVector}","page":"API reference","text":"clean_microbiome(tables::Vector{DataFrame}, administrations::AbstractVector;\n                 nokeymiss=true, namedict=nothing)\n\nClean HMB administrations: 1 = cohort 1 baseline, 2 = cohort 1 follow-up, 3 = cohort 2. Preserves one row per (name, cohort, administration) and rejects duplicate observations. Missing person/village IDs are dropped only when nokeymiss=true. The two-table method maps to administrations [1, 3].\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"HondurasTools.clean_microbiome"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#HondurasTools.clean_outcomes-Tuple{Vector{DataFrame}, Any}","page":"API reference","text":"clean_outcomes(dfs, waves; codebook = nothing, namedict = nothing, study = \"rct\")\n\nClean outcome/treatment DataFrames for the selected study (default \"rct\").\n\nStrips wave suffixes, combines waves, and renames respondent_master_id → name. If a codebook is provided, also:\n\nClassifies outcome columns by codebook outcome_type\nRecodes outcome columns to Union{Missing, Bool} via recode_outcome\nCategorizes village_code\nConverts resp_target, friend_treatment, household_target → Union{Missing, Bool}\n\ncodebook can be a NamedTuple from load_codebook() (preferred) or a bare DataFrame with columns variable_id and outcome_type (legacy).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"HondurasTools.clean_outcomes"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#HondurasTools.clean_perception!-Tuple{Any}","page":"API reference","text":"    clean_perception!(resp)\n\nClean the behavior/norm perception variables in the respondent data.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"HondurasTools.clean_perception!"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#HondurasTools.clean_respondent-Tuple{Vector{DataFrame}, Any}","page":"API reference","text":"    clean_respondent(\n        resp::Vector{DataFrame},\n        waves;\n        nokeymiss = false,\n        onlycomplete = false,\n        namedict = nothing\n    )\n\nClean the respondent level data. resp must be a vector of dataframes. Respondent data, resp, must be ordered by and match waves.\n\nCombines and processes.\n\nARGS ≡≡≡≡≡≡≡≡≡≡\n\nresp: a vector of DataFrames, with entries for each wave of the data.\nwaves: indicate the wave of each DataFrame in the same order as resp.\nnokeymiss: if true, do not allow entries with missing values for [:villagecode, :gender, :dateofbirth, :buildingid]\nonlycomplete: only include completed surveys\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"HondurasTools.clean_respondent"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#HondurasTools.code_variables!-Tuple{Any}","page":"API reference","text":"code_variables!(df; cb = nothing)\n\nApply categorical coding to cleaned columns in df. Hardcoded levels reflect post-cleaning string values, which often differ from raw survey option labels in the codebook (different case, simplified wording). Use make_ordered_categorical from cb for new variables not yet listed here.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"HondurasTools.code_variables!"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#HondurasTools.constraint-Tuple{Any, Any}","page":"API reference","text":"    constraint(g, i)\n\nDescription\n\nNetwork constraint as defined in Burt (1993). Here, larger constraint implies fewer structural holes (and, on the theory, less social capital).\n\nCurrently defined only for unweighted (binary) graphs.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"HondurasTools.constraint"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#HondurasTools.distance_df-NTuple{4, Any}","page":"API reference","text":"    distance_df(\n        dst, csspath, vdst, vgr;\n        a1 = :building_id_a1, a2 = :building_id_a2\n    )\n\nDescription\n\nN.B., a1 and a1 are whatever two node columns you want to find distances for (between the nodes).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"HondurasTools.distance_df"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#HondurasTools.dyadconstraint-Tuple{Any, Any, Any}","page":"API reference","text":"    dyadconstraint(g, i, j)\n\nDescription\n\nConstraint for (i,j), defined in Burt (1993).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"HondurasTools.dyadconstraint"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#HondurasTools.groupdescribe-Tuple{Any, Any}","page":"API reference","text":"    groupdescribe(df, grpvars)\n\nSeparately describe each group (by grpvars) of df, and concatenate.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"HondurasTools.groupdescribe"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#HondurasTools.grouppartition!-NTuple{4, Any}","page":"API reference","text":"    grouppartition!(ndf, rr, v)\n\nDetails\n\nndf is a networkinfo DataFrame, with names and village_code populated.\n\nCreate a partition vector from names in ndf over characteristic v, using input information cr.\n\nFor use with modularity calculation. N.B. further post processing likely required to coerce values into consecutive and distinct integers for groups.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"HondurasTools.grouppartition!"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#HondurasTools.hh_distances-Tuple{Any, Any}","page":"API reference","text":"    hh_distances(css, hc)\n\nCalculate and add household distances to the css data.\n\nWrites .bson file.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"HondurasTools.hh_distances"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#HondurasTools.hhwave-Tuple{Any, Any}","page":"API reference","text":"    hhwave(hd, noupd; ids = ids, wave = 4)\n\nHousehold data for a wave, with imputation except for noupd variables.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"HondurasTools.hhwave"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#HondurasTools.household_distances-Tuple{Any}","page":"API reference","text":"    household_distances(bdf4)\n\nDescription\n\nCalculate the household distances to be matched into an edgelist-style DataFrame (below).\n\nbdf4 is the household data (e.g., \"hondurashouseholdsWAVE4_v2.csv\").\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"HondurasTools.household_distances"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#HondurasTools.interlen-Tuple{Any, Any}","page":"API reference","text":"    interlen(x, y)\n\nReturn the length of the intersecting elements.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"HondurasTools.interlen"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#HondurasTools.invillage_adjust-Tuple{Any, Any}","page":"API reference","text":"    invillage_adjust(x, wi)\n\nAdjust invillage based on imputation.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"HondurasTools.invillage_adjust"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#HondurasTools.irrelreplace!-Tuple{Any, Any}","page":"API reference","text":"    irrelreplace!(cr, v; extra = String[])\n\nvalues equal to any of [\"Don't know\", \"Don't Know\", \"Dont_Know\", \"Refused\", \"Removed\"] become missing. Pass extra for dataset-specific additions (e.g., [\"NA\"]).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"HondurasTools.irrelreplace!"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#HondurasTools.join_ndf_cr!-Tuple{Any, Any}","page":"API reference","text":"joinndfcr!(     df, ndf; name = :perceiver, rels = [\"freetime\", \"personalprivate\"] )\n\nDescription\n\nJoin ndf network info DataFrame to cr CSS DataFrame.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"HondurasTools.join_ndf_cr!"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#HondurasTools.join_ndf_df!-Tuple{Any, Any}","page":"API reference","text":"joinndfdf!(df, ndf; name = :name)\n\nDescription\n\nJoin network info DataFrame ndf to respondent-level DataFrame df. This is not a simple join because ndf must be row-expanded as it is joined.\n\nndf should be filtered appropriately to the same number of waves, and no more than one relationship.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"HondurasTools.join_ndf_df!"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#HondurasTools.jointnetwork-NTuple{4, Any}","page":"API reference","text":"    jointnetwork(con, net_name1, net_name2, new_name; add_symmetric = false)\n\nARGS\n\ncon : connections data for multiple waves, villages. The function will filter to the relevant relationships (R and Q).\nnet_name1 : first relationship (R)\nnet_name2 : second relationship (Q)\nnew_name : name for new joint relationship (W)\n\nDescription\n\nTake two paired networks; e.g., borrow and lend money, and construct an edgelist (network) based on a compound relationship, for relationships R, Q and new relationship W: such that if (aRb) & (bQa), then aWb. The function will also note whether each (new) edge is (i) symmetric, and (ii) whether the alter in the edge also exists as an ego (N.B., using only the data in the new set. If you want to use the larger node-set run addsymmetric() on the full, combined, data). Return a dataframe with columns identical to con that contains the edges.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"HondurasTools.jointnetwork"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#HondurasTools.load_codebook-Tuple{}","page":"API reference","text":"load_codebook(; path = <bundled codebook dir>)\n\nLoad the three machine-readable codebook CSVs and return a NamedTuple containing:\n\nvariables, options, derivations — raw DataFrames\ngvariables, goptions — GroupedDataFrames keyed on [:study, :variable_id] for O(1) per-variable lookups\ngvar_by_type, gvar_by_level — GroupedDataFrames for type/level queries\noutcome_type_map — pre-built Dict{String,String} mapping base variable name to outcome_type, for unambiguous RCT classifications (compatibility view)\noutcome_type_index — classifications keyed by (study, base variable, wave); nothing denotes a wave-independent derivation\n\nThe bundled codebooks (at HondurasTools/codebook/) are the default. Pass path to override with an external directory.\n\nSchema\n\nvariables   — one row per variable-wave; fields: study, variableid, wave, level,                  variabletype, questiontype, survey, repeatedquestion, questiontexten, ...\noptions     — one row per variable-wave-option; fields: study, variableid, wave,                  optioncode, labelen, labelenmale, labeles, labelesmale\nderivations — one row per derived/outcome variable; fields: study, variableid,                  outcometype, denominator, derivation, source_questions, notes\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"HondurasTools.load_codebook"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#HondurasTools.make_ordered_categorical-Tuple{NamedTuple, AbstractString, Any}","page":"API reference","text":"make_ordered_categorical(cb, variable_id, data_col; wave = nothing, study = \"rct\")\n\nBuild an ordered CategoricalArray for data_col using option labels from cb.options, sorted by option_code. With wave=nothing, every wave must have identical labels and ordering; otherwise specify a wave explicitly.\n\nIntended for new variables not yet in code_variables!. For existing cleaned variables, verify that option labels match the cleaned values (raw labels may differ in case or wording from post-cleaning values).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"HondurasTools.make_ordered_categorical"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#HondurasTools.neighbordata-Tuple{Any}","page":"API reference","text":"    neighbordata(ndfw; additionals = nothing, flat = true)\n\nDescription\n\nadditionals: additional variables to include from ndfw\n\nExtract neighbor names from ndfw network info DataFrame.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"HondurasTools.neighbordata"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#HondurasTools.neighborprops!-Union{Tuple{S}, Tuple{Array{Vector{S}, 1}, AbstractGraph}} where S","page":"API reference","text":"    neighborprops!(\n        nb::Vector{Vector{S}}, g::AbstractGraph; property = :name\n    )\n\nDescription\n\nGet the property for each neighbor, for each node in the graph.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"HondurasTools.neighborprops!"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#HondurasTools.neighborprops!-Union{Tuple{S}, Tuple{T}, Tuple{Array{Array{Vector{S}, 1}, 1}, Vector{T}}} where {T<:AbstractGraph, S}","page":"API reference","text":"    neighborprops!(\n        nb::Vector{Vector{Vector{S}}}, gs::Vector{T}; property = :name\n    )\n\nDescription\n\nGet the property for each neighbor, for each node in the graph, for a vector of graphs.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"HondurasTools.neighborprops!"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#HondurasTools.neighborprops-Tuple{AbstractGraph, AbstractString}","page":"API reference","text":"    neighborprops(g::AbstractGraph, v::AbstractString; property = :name)\n\nDescription\n\nGet the specified property for each neighbor of v, where v is the property of that node.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"HondurasTools.neighborprops"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#HondurasTools.neighborprops-Tuple{AbstractGraph, Int64}","page":"API reference","text":"    neighborprops(g::AbstractGraph, v::Int; property = :name)\n\nDescription\n\nGet the specified property for each neighbor of v, where v is the vertex index in the graph object.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"HondurasTools.neighborprops"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#HondurasTools.network_info!-Tuple{Any, Any}","page":"API reference","text":"    network_info!(ndf, gcx, mods)\n\nPopulate ndf with network information.\n\nmods requires complex input that isn't really set up yet\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"HondurasTools.network_info!"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#HondurasTools.networkinfo-Tuple{Any}","page":"API reference","text":"networkinfo(     cx, cr;     waves = [1,3,4],     relnames = [\"freetime\", \"personalprivate\", \"kin\", \"union\", \"any\"],     alter_source = \"Census\" )\n\nDescription\n\ncx: connections data\nrr = nothing: reference DataFrame for modularity calculations\nmods = nothing: Vector of variables in rr to calculate modularity over. currently, only works for binary variables with missing values.\n\nConstruct a DataFrame that contains network statistics and graph objects from an input edgelist. N.B. that the edgelist should include all possible edges, which is filtered to the desired number of relationships. The larger set is needed to capture nodes that exist but do not have any ties in a particular network. Full sets are stored in names_all.\n\nN.B. that combination networks (and therefore relnames) is basically only usable as-is.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"HondurasTools.networkinfo"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#HondurasTools.prepare_for_bson-Tuple{AbstractDataFrame}","page":"API reference","text":"prepare_for_bson(df)\n\nConvert problematic column types to BSON-compatible formats. Handles all InlineStrings variants and other problematic types.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"HondurasTools.prepare_for_bson"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#HondurasTools.process_nvariable!-Tuple{DataFrame, Union{String, Symbol}, Function}","page":"API reference","text":"    process_nvariable!(\n        df::DataFrame, v::Union{Symbol, String}, stat::Function\n    )\n\nDescription\n\nCalculate number missing and a scalar statistic (e.g., the mean) over each entry of Vector{Union{Missing, Real}}\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"HondurasTools.process_nvariable!"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#HondurasTools.prp-Tuple{Any}","page":"API reference","text":"    prp(x; a=3, b=2)\n\nDescription\n\nsum(x .== a)/(sum(x .== b) + sum(x .== a))\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"HondurasTools.prp"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#HondurasTools.recode_outcome-Tuple{Any}","page":"API reference","text":"recode_outcome(vector; na = \"NA\", dk = 0, refused = 999)\n\nRecode a raw survey outcome vector to Union{Missing, Bool}.\n\nHandles both string columns (with \"NA\" values that force CSV.jl to parse as strings) and integer columns. The codebook convention is:\n\nna string → missing (not applicable / not surveyed)\ndk (default 0) → missing (don't know)\nrefused (default 999) → missing (refused)\n1 → false (negative outcome)\n2 → true (positive outcome)\n\nUses boolvec for the final [1,2] → Bool conversion.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"HondurasTools.recode_outcome"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#HondurasTools.respwave-Tuple{Any, Any}","page":"API reference","text":"Respondent data for a wave, with imputation except for noupd variables.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"HondurasTools.respwave"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#HondurasTools.reversestandard-Tuple{AbstractDataFrame, Any, Any}","page":"API reference","text":"    reversestandard(df, v, transforms)\n\nDescription\n\nReverse standardization for a column and a set of transforms. Non-mutating.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"HondurasTools.reversestandard"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#HondurasTools.reversestandard-Tuple{AbstractVector, Any, Any}","page":"API reference","text":"    reversestandard(x, v, transforms)\n\nDescription\n\nReverse standardization for a column and a set of transforms. Non-mutating.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"HondurasTools.reversestandard"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#HondurasTools.reversestandards!-Tuple{Any, Any, Any}","page":"API reference","text":"    reversestandards!(df, vbls, transforms; digits = nothing)\n\nDescription\n\nReverse the data standardization for columns present in transforms and in vbls.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"HondurasTools.reversestandards!"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#HondurasTools.reversestandards!-Tuple{Any, Any}","page":"API reference","text":"    reversestandards!(df, transforms; digits = nothing)\n\nDescription\n\nReverse the data standardization for all columns present in transforms.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"HondurasTools.reversestandards!"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#HondurasTools.sa-Tuple{Any}","page":"API reference","text":"    sa(a)\n\nSkip missing and NaN. via Skipper.jl.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"HondurasTools.sa"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#HondurasTools.sai-Tuple{Any}","page":"API reference","text":"    sai(a)\n\nSkip Inf and NaN. via Skipper.jl.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"HondurasTools.sai"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#HondurasTools.stround-Tuple{Any}","page":"API reference","text":"    stround(x; digits = 2)\n\nDescription\n\nReturn String rounded to d digits for numeric/integer.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"HondurasTools.stround"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#HondurasTools.sunique-Tuple{Any}","page":"API reference","text":"    sunique(x)\n\nReturn sorted unique object.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"HondurasTools.sunique"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#HondurasTools.tiestrength-Tuple{Any, Any, Any}","page":"API reference","text":"    tiestrength(g, i, j)\n\nBidirectional measure\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"HondurasTools.tiestrength"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#HondurasTools.transformunitvalues-Tuple{Any}","page":"API reference","text":"Using the ZScore convenience fn. to do UnitRange transformation\n\nfor use with StandardizedPredictors.jl\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"HondurasTools.transformunitvalues"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#HondurasTools.tryindex-Union{Tuple{T}, Tuple{T, Any, Any}} where T<:AbstractMetaGraph","page":"API reference","text":"    tryindex(g::T, a, prop; alt = NaN)\n\nDescription\n\nTry to return index from property for MetaGraph, return NaN otherwise.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"HondurasTools.tryindex"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#HondurasTools.tuple_addinv-Tuple{Any}","page":"API reference","text":"    tuple_addinv(c)\n\nDescription\n\nReplace tuple (v, v) with tuple (1-v, 1-v).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"HondurasTools.tuple_addinv"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#HondurasTools.unilen-Tuple{Any}","page":"API reference","text":"    unilen(x)\n\nReturn the number of unique elements.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"HondurasTools.unilen"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#HondurasTools.upck-Tuple{Any}","page":"API reference","text":"upck(x)\n\nReturn the last element of a vector or give missing if length < 1.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"HondurasTools.upck"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#HondurasTools.variable_info-Tuple{NamedTuple, AbstractString}","page":"API reference","text":"variable_info(cb, varname; wave = nothing, study = \"rct\")\n\nReturn all rows in cb.variables for varname, optionally filtered by wave and study.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"HondurasTools.variable_info"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#HondurasTools.variable_options-Tuple{NamedTuple, AbstractString}","page":"API reference","text":"variable_options(cb, varname; wave = nothing, study = \"rct\")\n\nReturn option codes and labels for varname from cb.options, sorted by option_code.\n\nNote: option_code reflects survey display order, which may differ from the desired analytical ordering for ordered categoricals. Use the returned labels as a starting point and reorder manually if needed.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"HondurasTools.variable_options"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#HondurasTools.variables_by_level-Tuple{NamedTuple, AbstractString}","page":"API reference","text":"variables_by_level(cb, level; study = \"rct\")\n\nReturn unique variable_ids at a given data level for the given study.\n\nLevels: \"respondent\", \"household\", \"village\", \"connection\", \"outcome\"\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"HondurasTools.variables_by_level"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#HondurasTools.variables_by_type-Tuple{NamedTuple, AbstractString}","page":"API reference","text":"variables_by_type(cb, vtype; study = \"rct\")\n\nReturn unique variable_ids where variable_type == vtype for the given study.\n\nCommon types: \"survey\", \"derived\", \"admin\", \"treatment\", \"identifier\", \"outcome\",               \"intro\", \"flag\"\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"HondurasTools.variables_by_type"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#HondurasTools.villwave-Tuple{Any, Any}","page":"API reference","text":"Village data for a wave, with imputation except for noupd variables.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"HondurasTools.villwave"},{"category":"section","location":"processing/#Processing-and-analysis","page":"Processing and analysis","text":"","title":"Processing and analysis"},{"category":"section","location":"processing/#Inputs-and-outputs","page":"Processing and analysis","text":"make_data.jl is a delivery-specific template. Supply one DataFrame per wave and a corresponding wave vector, in the same order. The RCT cleaners combine wave tables after harmonizing columns; uncollected variables can remain missing. Missingness alone does not establish whether a question was asked: consult the study-specific codebook.\n\nusing HondurasTools\nwaves = [1, 2, 3]\npaths = [\"raw/respondents_w1.csv\", \"raw/respondents_w2.csv\", \"raw/respondents_w3.csv\"]\nraw = [CSV.read(path, DataFrame; missingstring=\"NA\") for path in paths]\nresp = clean_respondent(raw, waves; nokeymiss=false, onlycomplete=false)\nsubset!(resp, :data_source => ByRow(==(\"Census, survey\")))\n\nPaths above are illustrative. The template also processes household, village, connection, and microbiome tables and writes one dated CSV per level. It does not automatically join those outputs.","title":"Inputs and outputs"},{"category":"section","location":"processing/#Cleaning-defaults","page":"Processing and analysis","text":"Function Default behavior and constraints\nclean_respondent(resp, waves) Does not require complete surveys or nonmissing key fields by default. Recodes data_source to text labels and respondent_master_id to name.\nclean_household(hh, waves) Drops rows missing village_code or building_id by default.\nclean_village(vdfs, waves) Harmonizes and recodes village tables; assumes expected delivery columns.\nclean_connections(conns, waves) Filters to the same village by default; alter_source filtering is off by default.\nclean_microbiome(mb1, mb2) Adds cohort indicators, retains the union of cohort columns (absent values become missing), and drops missing village/person identifiers by default.\nclean_outcomes(dfs, waves) Combines tables and renames person IDs. Outcome recoding requires codebook=cb.\nclean_ihr(cop, ihr) Cleans cooperation and IH tables; requires the expected full schema.\n\nnokeymiss=true in clean_respondent removes rows missing village code, gender, date of birth, or building ID. onlycomplete=true restricts to completed surveys. Neither is enabled in the template.\n\nFor connections, same_village=true drops missing same-village indicators and keeps code 1. alter_source=true keeps raw code 1. removemissing=true calls disallowmissing! on ego, alter, question, alter source, and relationship: it rejects remaining missing values rather than dropping all incomplete rows. Required columns must still be present even when filtering is disabled. The cleaner converts source codes to labels after filtering.\n\nSome recoding operations check whether variables exist, but other operations access columns unconditionally. Support for arbitrary subsets of requested variables is not guaranteed.","title":"Cleaning defaults"},{"category":"section","location":"processing/#Identifiers-and-joins","page":"Processing and analysis","text":"Cleaned respondent IDs use name; household processing uses building_id. Do not assume a building is a stable household across waves. Include wave and village identifiers where the intended unit requires them, and check key uniqueness before joining. The respondent cleaner removes the legacy household_id column.\n\nMicrobiome data are separate from the RCT wave tables. Choose and document how to handle changed villages between administrations before joining on person and village. The package includes codebook/microbiome_villages.csv; study-specific sample restrictions remain analysis decisions.\n\nAn edgelist omits people without recorded ties. Construct the vertex population from the appropriate respondent roster when isolates matter, and apply compatible inclusion criteria to both tables.","title":"Identifiers and joins"},{"category":"section","location":"processing/#Further-analysis","page":"Processing and analysis","text":"Longitudinal utilities include respprocess, householdprocess, and villageprocess, with respwave, hhwave, and villwave for wave-specific outputs. Their carry-forward and variable-selection choices require review for the analysis; do not assume every variable is time-invariant.\n\nNetwork utilities include jointnetwork, addsymmetric!, tiestrength, constraint, and neighbor-data helpers. Inspect their API documentation and required graph/table structure before use. The old src/process_edgelist.jl file is not included by the module and is not a supported exported entry point.","title":"Further analysis"},{"category":"section","location":"processing/#Validation-and-partial-responses","page":"Processing and analysis","text":"Donation amounts in microbiome data accept numeric integers and integer strings. Missing or unparseable text becomes missing; fractional numeric amounts raise an error. The donation column may be absent.\n\nOutcome treatment flags preserve missing values and require nonmissing codes to be 0 or 1. Known OCR errors in outcome classifications are normalized by the codebook loader; unknown classifications raise an error rather than silently skipping recoding.\n\nFor a terminal risk response without its preceding-stage response, the terminal risk score is retained while the unavailable green and purple scores remain missing. This does not reconstruct unobserved choices.","title":"Validation and partial responses"},{"category":"section","location":"processing/#Repeated-HMB-administrations","page":"Processing and analysis","text":"The two-table call still means cohort 1 baseline and cohort 2. For follow-up data, specify the codebook administration IDs explicitly:\n\nmb = clean_microbiome([baseline, followup, cohort2], [1, 2, 3])\n\nOutputs retain cohort and administration. Person/cohort/administration is the observation key, including for risk scoring and its join back to survey data. Repeated people across administrations are retained; duplicate observations within an administration raise an error. Inputs to this cleaner are copied. Missing identifiers are dropped with nokeymiss=true; otherwise records remain, but missing person identifiers are not assigned risk scores. Do not join repeated observations on person alone.","title":"Repeated HMB administrations"},{"category":"section","location":"processing/#Input-contracts","page":"Processing and analysis","text":"Wave-based cleaners require a nonempty table list, a matching vector of distinct integer RCT waves 1–4, and structural columns in each table. Wave suffixes may be present on required columns where the cleaner supports suffix removal. Missing required columns raise an ArgumentError naming the cleaner and columns.\n\nCleaner Required input columns\nRespondent respondent_master_id, village_code, building_id, data_source, complete, survey_start, date_of_birth, gender\nHousehold Person, village, and building IDs per table; combined delivery also needs l0400, l0500, l1000, l1100, l1500, l1600, village_name, municipality, office\nConnections village_code, ego, alter, question, relationship, alter_source, same_building, same_village, a2701, a2702\nOutcomes respondent_master_id; outcome and treatment columns may be absent\nHMB respondent_master_id, village_code, lives_in_village, works_in_village; donation and other guarded survey items may be absent\nVillage Village ID and wealth-index columns; combined delivery also requires cemetery/location, electricity, and summary columns used by the cleaner\nIH/cooperation The full delivery schema; validation lists missing columns explicitly\n\nThese checks validate column availability, not every value or every conditional dependency. A present optional field can still require a companion field (for example, HMB cognitive score and cognitive status).","title":"Input contracts"},{"category":"section","location":"development/#Development-and-documentation","page":"Development","text":"From the repository root, install the package into the documentation environment:\n\njulia --project=docs -e 'using Pkg; Pkg.develop(path=pwd()); Pkg.instantiate()'\njulia --project=docs docs/make.jl\n\nThe generated site is written to docs/build/. Local builds do not deploy. CI uses the same environment and enables deployment only on push events, using the repository's configured documentation credentials.\n\nRun package tests separately:\n\njulia --project=. -e 'using Pkg; Pkg.test()'\n\nDocumentation builds validate docstring references and page generation, not raw-data cleaning against a particular delivery. Review required columns, row counts, identifiers, missingness, and category levels when adapting the template. Keep raw participant data and generated analysis datasets outside version control.\n\nPackage-test CI runs on Julia 1.10, 1.11, 1.12, and the latest stable 1.x release. Synthetic regression tests cover multi-wave outcomes, study isolation, repeated HMB administrations, missing IDs, and risk-score joins; no participant data are needed.","title":"Development and documentation"},{"category":"section","location":"#HondurasTools.jl","page":"Home","text":"HondurasTools cleans and combines Honduras survey deliveries and provides codebook, longitudinal, and network analysis helpers.\n\nStart with Processing and analysis for the data workflow, Codebooks and provenance for metadata, and API reference for function documentation. Installation and a runnable template are described in the repository README.\n\nThe package does not distribute raw participant data. Examples requiring survey tables must be adapted to your local delivery. Cleaner input tables may be mutated; use deepcopy when the original tables must be retained.","title":"HondurasTools.jl"}]
}
